   "fix": "Give the one primary action a real button in the first screen (a filled or solid-bordered control, 44px or taller), not only a link in the navigation.",
   "alsoAt": [
    768,
    390
   ]
  },
  {
   "level": "FAIL",
   "id": "focal-point",
   "width": 1440,
   "message": "The first screen has no dominant element: the largest type is 32px (2.2vw) and no single picture holds a third of the viewport.",
   "fix": "Make one thing lead: the headline at display size, or one large picture. A grid of many small pictures is not one thing; in blind tests jurors marked a first screen without a headline down every time. On the strongest studied sites the largest type has a median of 8.4vw.",
   "alsoAt": [
    768
   ]
  },
  {
   "level": "WARN",
   "id": "off-centre",
   "width": 1440,
   "message": "The margins differ: 120px on the left, 137px on the right, at 1440px.",
   "fix": "Put the navigation and the content on one pair of margins; check for padding on one side only, a scrollbar gutter, or a header on another inset than the page.",
   "alsoAt": [
    768,
    390
   ]
  },
  {
   "level": "WARN",
   "id": "name-only-title",
   "width": 1440,
   "message": "The h1 is a bare name (\"GymClues in action\") and nothing else on the first screen says what this is.",
   "fix": "Keep the name if it leads, and give the line under it (18px or more) the plain words: what it is, for whom, where or when. In the study of 97 Sites of the Day, first screens that named nothing lost usability and content points.",
   "alsoAt": [
    768,
    390
   ]
  },
